hi there we had this question on our ai  coffee break quiz recently and we must  discuss its answer here because we never  had such a contested question before on  our quiz so if you do not know what i'm  talking about here follow miss coffee  bean to the community tab of this  channel because we have quite the set of  machine learning related quiz questions  and the new question is coming each day  but let's get to our question about the  sequence length of the transformer it's  not a simple question not gonna lie the  answer depends a bit on how one reads it  christian garcia noted very well that  transformers do not process just  sequences but something more general a  sequence without order which is a set  the sequential nature of the data  becomes clear to the transformer only  because we add positional embeddings to  encode the order in which the data comes  in if it is the case for more about this  check out some of our previous videos  but supposing we are processing a  sequence with a given model for example  sequence of words does the sequence  always have to have the same length or  can this length vary  and it's a valid question because there  are architectures like convolutional  architectures with a fully connected  layer in the end where the input must  have always the same size if the image  is not the right size it gets resized  during pre-processing so we want to  answer this question for the transformer  in two ways first by looking into the  code and then theoretically because well  one just does not need to play around a  lot with a transformer from hugging  phase to notice that inputs which have  different lengths are possible so yes  the things we want to process with a  transformer can have variable lengths  the tokenizer just gives us more token  ids as output and an attention mask full  of ones but with more ones as the  sequence grows bigger but because of the  quadratically scaling attention in  compute we also notice parameters in the  transformers library limiting the  sequence length which has the effect of  truncating everything which exceeds that  length to keep memory and computation in  bounds looking at the bird tokenizer  documentation we also see the pad token  and its explanation by padding the  sequence to a maximum length we can  produce inputs of fixed length for the  transformer even when the sequence is  shorter than the maximum length our  input length has always the same size  because the sequence is padded with the  pad token and the attention mask is  padded with zeros and all this trouble  is for efficiency reasons in the  implementation when we want to train or  do inference on more than just one  sample at once because in that case  tensors in a batch should have the same  size such that the gpu can handle this  efficiently therefore the answer to this  question of whether transformers process  sequences of a predefined length or a  variable length is both yes and no  yes sequence length can vary as in yes  the transformer can process any length  but also  no because efficient implementations fix  the length to a maximum length anyway  but in principle and i think that it is  the most important thing about  transformers if everything with  positional encodings and training works  well for every sequence length there's  no reason for why transformers should be  bound to a specific set size or sequence  length because the feed forward layers  we see here are the same no matter the  position of the token we are looking at  so even if we add here one more token at  the end this does not care as we take  the same feed forward module and its  weights and apply them on the newly  added tokens and the attention is also  not caring about the sequence length as  it just computes a pairwise score given  a vector and another vectors so it just  looks at vector pairs at a time  therefore miss coffee bean would rather  say that transformers process sequences  of variable length in principle